Q = lambda q, opts, ans, why, src: {"q": q, "opts": opts, "ans": ans, "why": why, "src": src}

LESSONS = [
L("Chapter 1 — The Stock Entry & its purposes", "13", "<p>Module 1 built the map; this module moves stock across it. The instrument is the <b>Stock Entry</b> — the document family for movements that have <b>no commercial counterparty</b>: nothing is being bought or sold; stock is simply appearing, disappearing, or changing rooms inside your own custody. One doctype, several <b>purposes</b>, and the purpose — exactly as it was on the Material Request — decides the machinery.</p><ul><li><b>Material Receipt</b> — stock IN with no supplier transaction behind it: the genuine appearance (found stock surfacing at a count's edge, promotional goods arriving free, and — carefully policed — certain opening flows). Because value enters with quantity, the receipt DEMANDS a valuation rate: Chapter 4 owns the discipline.</li><li><b>Material Issue</b> — stock OUT with no customer: internal consumption (the cleaning supplies, the spare parts), and value leaving deliberately (the write-off of expired or damaged goods). The out-going value lands somewhere in the books, which makes every issue an expense event wearing warehouse clothes: Chapter 3.</li><li><b>Material Transfer</b> — stock BETWEEN two of your own leaves: same total, different rooms, custody handed over. One-step for the trivial case (Chapter 5), two-step through the transit room for real distance (Chapter 6).</li><li><b>Repack</b> — stock TRANSFORMED: consume these items, produce those, value flowing from inputs to outputs — the bulk carton becoming retail strips without a subcontractor (Chapter 7).</li></ul><p><b>The shared anatomy.</b> Whatever the purpose: a posting date and time (the chronology law from module 1 — this document writes the ledger, so its date is testimony); rows of item, quantity, UOM with its conversion (the arithmetic never rests); source warehouse, target warehouse, or both, per the purpose's shape; and the <b>reason</b> — the remark or reference that answers, years later, WHY this movement happened. A trading document's why is its counterparty and its chain; a stock entry's why is a sentence a human wrote, which is precisely why Chapter 2's boundary matters and why an unexplained stock entry is this track's version of an unsigned cheque.</p><p><b>Submission's weight.</b> A submitted stock entry writes the ledger — ins, outs, valuation effects — with everything module 1 taught about that act: irreversible in the ordinary course, correctable only by appended counter-movements, and evidence forever. Draft, check the purpose, the rooms, the quantities, the rate where one is demanded, and the reason; then submit. The whole module is the elaboration of that checklist.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The boundary law: what stock entries are NOT for", "14", "<p>Before the legitimate uses, the conviction that protects them — because the Stock Entry is the most abusable document in the system, and the abuse is always the same one.</p><p><b>The law: trading movements never travel by stock entry.</b> Goods bought arrive by Purchase Receipt against a Purchase Order; goods sold leave by Delivery Note against a Sales Order; returns travel by their return documents. The Stock Entry is for movements with <b>no counterparty</b> — and the moment there is a supplier or a customer on the other side of the goods, using a stock entry amputates everything both trading tracks built:</p><ul><li><b>Receive a purchase as Material Receipt</b> and: no PO fulfilment counts down, the three-way match loses its possession leg (the invoice will match nothing — Procurement 7's control disarmed at birth), the supplier's delivery performance vanishes from the record, landed cost has no receipt to attach to, and the valuation rate is whatever a human typed instead of the committed rate travelling. One shortcut, five amputations.</li><li><b>Ship a sale as Material Issue</b> and: no order fulfilment moves, no invoice chain exists, the customer's ledger never learns, serial and batch outbound custody breaks, and the revenue the goods represent has left the building as an EXPENSE. This is not a workaround; it is the shape unrecorded sales take.</li></ul><p><b>Why the abuse happens — and the honest answers.</b> The stock entry is FAST: no master needed, no chain, no match — which is exactly the problem, and exactly why it tempts at the busy moments. The situations that drive it, each with its lawful answer: <i>the supplier isn't set up yet</i> — set them up (Procurement 1's gate exists for this, and the emergency onboarding is an hour, not a week); <i>we just need the stock in the system NOW</i> — the emergency PO-and-receipt is minutes, and it keeps the chain (Procurement 6's unordered-truck doctrine: even urgency gets a document); <i>it's a tiny cash purchase</i> — the policy's exempted tail exists, written, and even the tail buys through a minimal purchase flow, not a warehouse side door.</p><p><b>The consultant's detection kit.</b> The abuse announces itself in the ledger: Material Receipts with valuation rates suspiciously like supplier prices, Material Issues tracking suspiciously with sales patterns, stock entries clustering at month-end or at one user. The standing audit — stock entries reviewed monthly by purpose, value, and author (Chapter 9 makes it a calendar item) — is cheap; the archaeology after a year of side-door trading is not: unmatched invoices, unmeasurable suppliers, valuation built on typed guesses, and a stock asset the auditor rightly distrusts.</p><p><b>The law's positive face.</b> Held properly, the boundary makes BOTH families trustworthy: every trading movement carries its chain (matchable, measurable, valued by its documents), and every stock entry means what it says — a genuinely internal event, explained by its reason. A ledger where the two never blur is a ledger where every line's ancestry is knowable from its document type alone — which is the property every diagnosis in this track leans on.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Material Issue: consumption & the write-off", "13", "<p>Stock leaves without a customer for two honest reasons — it was USED, or it must be DESTROYED — and the Material Issue serves both, with different governance for each.</p><p><b>Consumption — the routine issue.</b> The cleaning supplies to the floor, the stationery to admin, the spare part to the generator: stock consumed by the firm's own operation. The mechanics: issue FROM the custody that holds it (the Facilities store module 1 gave a room to), quantities in honest UOMs, and the value landing in the right expense context — because an issue is stock value becoming expense, and WHERE it lands (the expense attribution the accountant configures) is what makes consumption visible by department and category instead of dissolving into one anonymous bucket. The operating rhythm: issues recorded AT consumption (the weekly batch that fictionalises Tuesday's usage into Friday's entry is the posting-date disease again), and the consumables reorder loop closing through the same planning fields as everything else — the Facilities store has bins too.</p><p><b>The write-off — value leaving with a signature.</b> Expired stock after the disposal decision, damage beyond negotiation, the quarantine lot whose fate (Procurement 6's fork) resolved to destruction: the Material Issue writes it out of stock and into the write-off's expense home. The governance is proportional to the fact that this is <b>company value being destroyed on someone's say-so</b>: a reason on the document always (the expiry batch numbers, the damage description, the reference to the quarantine decision); an approval line by value (the storekeeper alone does not write off ₦2m — thresholds, like every gate number in these tracks, written); physical destruction actually performed and, for regulated goods, performed per the compliance requirements (pharma disposal has rules, and the write-off document is part of that evidence file, not a substitute for it); and the identity trail closed — expired BATCHES written off by lot, so the batch's biography ends with a documented death, not a disappearance.</p><p><b>The issue is not a dustbin.</b> The disease this chapter must pre-empt: the Material Issue as the place unexplained differences go to die — the count that came up short quietly issued out as consumption, shrinkage laundered into expense lines a little every week. The tells: issue volumes trending with no operational story, issues without references, one author issuing everything. The law: <b>differences are for the reconciliation</b> (module 4) — counted, investigated, adjusted in the open with their own document and their own trend line; consumption is for things actually consumed. Blur them and the firm loses its shrinkage number, which means losing the ability to manage the very leak being hidden.</p><p><b>Reading issues as management.</b> Done honestly, the issue history is a report the owner wants: consumables cost by month and department, write-off value by reason (expiry vs damage — the first indicts buying quantities, the second indicts handling), and the trend that says whether the generator eats more spares this year than last. The document that records value leaving is also the document that explains it — the whole track's philosophy at one desk.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Material Receipt: stock from nowhere, valued honestly", "13", "<p>Stock appearing without a supplier is rare, legitimate, and dangerous in exact proportion to how casually it is valued. This chapter is the short list of honest uses and the one discipline they share.</p><p><b>The honest list.</b> <b>Found stock</b>: the count's edge case — goods physically present that the ledger never knew (the pallet behind the racking, the un-receipted sample that got shelved). Where reconciliation (module 4) is running, found stock usually enters THERE, inside the count's own document; the Material Receipt serves the between-counts discovery that cannot wait. <b>Genuine free goods</b>: promotional stock from a manufacturer with no invoice ever coming — real appearance, no counterparty payable. <b>Opening flows</b>, carefully: go-live stock loading is module 4's set-piece (the reconciliation is the proper instrument, and the Topmost-style disaster — opening stock keyed at selling prices — is convicted there in full); the Material Receipt appears in opening work only for narrow, deliberate cases, and never as the bulk loader.</p><p><b>The discipline: valuation is mandatory and honest.</b> Stock in means value in — the ledger will not take quantity without cost, so the Material Receipt demands a <b>valuation rate</b> per row. The rate is a statement the books will carry forever, so it is derived, not invented: found stock enters at what it would have cost (the item's current valuation or last honest purchase rate — the found pallet of PARA-500 enters at PARA-500's cost, not at zero and not at retail); free goods enter at the value policy the accountant sets (a zero-cost entry is defensible and distorts margin the day the goods sell at full price; a fair-cost entry with the offset the accountant directs is often truer — the point is that this is an ACCOUNTING choice made once, not a warehouse improvisation made per pallet); and every receipt carries its reason and reference, because stock-from-nowhere without a story is exactly what an auditor is trained to circle.</p><p><b>The valuation ripple, previewed.</b> Module 5 teaches the mechanics; plant the intuition now: the entering rate joins the item's valuation stream — under moving average it blends into every subsequent issue's cost; under FIFO it queues at its place. A careless rate on a large receipt therefore poisons COGS for weeks after: the zero-valued found pallet makes the next month's margins look brilliant and mean nothing. The bigger the quantity, the more the rate deserves a second pair of eyes — value thresholds on Material Receipts route to approval exactly as write-offs do, and for the same reason read in reverse: this is company value being CREATED on someone's say-so.</p><p><b>Frequency is the health check.</b> Material Receipts should be RARE. A firm whose ledger shows them weekly has one of three problems: trading through the side door (Chapter 2's abuse — check the rates and patterns), a broken receiving gate upstream (purchases failing into workarounds — fix the gate, not the symptom), or counts so infrequent that found-stock discoveries pile up between them (module 4's cadence answer). The document's proper life is boring: a handful of well-explained appearances a year, each with a rate someone could defend and a story someone recorded.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Material Transfer: the one-step move", "13", "<p>The simplest movement: stock changes rooms, custody changes hands, nothing else changes. The one-step Material Transfer serves the case where the move is effectively INSTANT — and knowing where that case ends is the chapter's real lesson.</p><p><b>The mechanics.</b> Rows carry item, quantity, <b>source warehouse and target warehouse</b>; submission writes both sides at once — OUT of the source, IN to the target, the same moment, the same document. Value travels with the goods at the source's valuation (a transfer never revalues — the goods cost what they cost regardless of the room; module 5 formalises it), identities travel by name (the serials and batches move as themselves, their biographies gaining a line), and the two bins update together: no gap, no limbo, conservation of stock in one document.</p><p><b>Where one-step is right.</b> The moves with no meaningful in-between: within one site (Central floor to Central quarantine after a damage discovery; the fork's manual edition), between adjacent custodies with instant handover (the back store to the sales floor where both are modelled), and any move where dispatch and receipt are the SAME physical act by people standing in the same place. The test: <i>is there a moment when the goods are with neither custodian?</i> No — one-step. Yes — Chapter 6, because that moment needs a room.</p><p><b>The custody handshake.</b> Even instant transfers move accountability, and the small disciplines keep the handshake real: the receiving side acknowledges (in a same-site move this is trivially the same team, but between custodians — floor to warehouse, store to store-within-store — the transfer done TO someone who did not know is how differences get born arguing); quantities counted at handover where the custodians differ (thirty seconds now versus a variance dispute at the next count); and the reason field carrying the why for anything non-routine (the damage transfer to quarantine references what happened — the quarantine review will want it).</p><p><b>What one-step must not stretch to cover.</b> The classic misuse: the Lagos-to-Ibadan transfer entered as one-step because it is fewer clicks. The document happily complies — OUT of Central, IN to Ibadan, the same second — and the ledger now says Ibadan holds stock that is, in the world, on a truck in Ogun State for the next six hours. Every read of Ibadan's bin is wrong until the truck arrives; a count at Ibadan during the window would surprise; and if the truck never arrives, the loss is discovered as an Ibadan shortage — the wrong custody blamed, the investigation starting a room away from the truth. Distance means a custody gap; the custody gap means the transit room; the transit room means the two-step — which is the next chapter, and the reason module 1 built the room before this module needed it.</p><p><b>Volume hygiene.</b> Transfers are the highest-frequency stock entries in a branch network, so their smallest frictions compound: templated rows for the routine weekly moves, defaults per flow (module 1's wiring), and the discipline that transfers are entered by the people MAKING them at the time of making — the pile of transfer paperwork keyed by one clerk on Friday is the posting-date disease at network scale.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The two-step transfer & the in-transit room", "14", "<p>Real distance separates dispatch from receipt, and the two-step transfer is how the system tells the truth about the in-between. This is the mechanical heart of the branch network (module 6 builds the governance; here is the machinery).</p><p><b>The two documents.</b> <b>Step one — dispatch</b>: Central sends 40 cartons to Ibadan; the transfer moves the goods OUT of Central Store and IN to the <b>In-Transit</b> room (module 1's warehouse with no walls). Central's custody has honestly ended; Ibadan's has honestly not begun; the transit room answers for the truck. <b>Step two — receipt</b>: the goods arrive; Ibadan's receiving transfer moves them OUT of In-Transit and IN to Ibadan Store — <b>at the quantities Ibadan actually counts</b>. The receiving side counts exactly as Procurement 6's gate taught, because an inter-branch arrival is a receiving event in every way that matters: count before accepting custody, against the dispatch document, discrepancies recorded as they are.</p><p><b>Why the room changes everything.</b> At every moment, one custody answers: before dispatch, Central; on the road, In-Transit; after receipt, Ibadan. The one-step's lie (Chapter 5) is structurally impossible — no bin ever claims goods it does not hold. And the failure cases become VISIBLE instead of misattributed: the truck that arrives short leaves the shortfall IN the transit room — a named, valued, investigable difference between what left and what arrived, sitting in the exact custody where the loss occurred — rather than surfacing weeks later as an Ibadan count variance blamed on the wrong storekeeper. Transit shrinkage is real in Nigerian logistics; the two-step is what makes it a managed number with a location instead of an argument between branches.</p><p><b>The transit disciplines.</b> Three, all cheap: <b>reference discipline</b> — dispatch and receipt documents linked (the receipt made against the dispatch, remainder logic pre-filling exactly as every create-from in these tracks), the truck's waybill referenced, so the road has a paper trail; <b>the aging read</b> — the transit room's balance, aged: anything older than the longest honest journey is an incident (truck delayed, receipt never entered, or goods gone), worked from a standing list — module 1 planted this diagnostic, and here is its operation: the transit room returns to zero between runs in a healthy network, and its aging report is the earliest smoke detector the network has; <b>the shortfall protocol</b> — arrival differences investigated from the transit balance while the trail is hot (the driver, the waybill, the seals), resolved by the honest instrument: recovered goods received, confirmed losses written off FROM transit with the write-off's governance (Chapter 3 — value leaving with a signature), never quietly absorbed by receiving inflated quantities to make the room zero out.</p><p><b>Receiving discipline at the branch.</b> The arriving branch signs for custody, so the gate rules apply in miniature: count against the dispatch note, not the driver's word; accept what arrived, record what did not; identities confirmed (the batches that left are the batches that arrived — the swap en route is a real fraud pattern, and batch-level receiving is what catches it). A branch that receives casually inherits every road-side difference as its own unexplained variance — the framing (module 1's custodian's number) that makes branch teams WANT the discipline.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Repack: transformation without a factory", "13", "<p>Between moving stock and manufacturing it sits a common trading need: the SAME goods in a DIFFERENT form — the bulk carton become retail strips, the 25kg sack become 1kg packs, the assorted lot broken into singles. The <b>Repack</b> purpose serves it, and its craft is mostly about value and honesty.</p><p><b>The mechanics.</b> One stock entry, two kinds of row: <b>consumed</b> rows (the inputs, OUT of their warehouse — one bulk carton of 100 strips) and <b>produced</b> rows (the outputs, IN — 100 retail strip units, plus, where honest, the packaging materials consumed alongside). Value flows conserved: the outputs enter at the inputs' total value (allocated across produced rows — by the sensible basis where multiple outputs), so no value is created or destroyed by changing the box. Quantity changes; UOM changes; identity persists where tracked — the produced strips carry the source batch and its expiry, because repacking a batch does not launder its provenance (the NAFDAC chain survives the scissors, and the entry's mapping is what proves it).</p><p><b>The yield honesty.</b> Real repacking has losses — the torn strip, the spillage from the sack — and the honest entry records the yield as it happened: 100 in, 98 saleable out, 2 to the damage row (written off with Chapter 3's governance, or quarantined per policy). The dishonest patterns to refuse: producing 100 from 98's worth of reality (the shortfall surfacing at the next count, blamed on custody), and the standing yield loss never trended (repack loss is a rate to WATCH — a packing team whose 2% becomes 5% is a conversation the trend triggers, exactly the subcontractor yield logic of Procurement 8 brought in-house).</p><p><b>Where repack ends and other machinery begins.</b> The boundary judgment, three ways: <b>repack vs subcontract</b> — the transformation done by an OUTSIDE party with your materials is Procurement 8's subcontract flow (service purchased, supplier-side custody), not a repack entry pretending the repacker doesn't exist; <b>repack vs manufacturing</b> — combining DIFFERENT materials into a genuinely new product (formulation, assembly with recipes, work-in-progress stages) is the manufacturing module's terrain, with its own masters and costing — the repack entry stretched over real manufacturing produces costs nobody can defend; <b>repack vs UOM</b> — and the subtlest: if the carton and the strip are the SAME item in different UOMs with a clean conversion, much selling and stocking needs no repack at all (Buying 2's UOM architecture already lets one item live in both units) — the repack exists for where the retail form is a DIFFERENT item (own code, own barcode, own price list line), which is an item-architecture decision made deliberately at setup, not per entry. The consultant who can walk a client through those three boundaries in ten minutes has saved them either a missing module or a pile of needless entries.</p><p><b>Governance, briefly.</b> Repack entries create sellable retail stock from bulk, so the same value-threshold eyes apply: reasons and references routine, the yield trend on the monthly read, and the mapping of batches through the transformation spot-checked — because the entry that says which batch became which strips is, for regulated goods, a compliance document wearing warehouse clothes.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: a month of movements at HealthTrade", "16", "<p>The network module 1 designed now runs a month of internal movements. Every chapter clocks in; every movement names its document.</p><p><b>Week 1 — the routine floor (Ch. 3).</b> Facilities issues run at consumption: cleaning supplies Tuesday, stationery Thursday, each landing in its expense attribution — the month-end consumables read will show admin ₦38k, floor ₦52k, and the MD will see consumption by department for the first time. One write-off: a water-damaged carton discovered shelf-side, transferred one-step to Central Quarantine (Ch. 5 — same site, instant custody), then written off after the weekly quarantine review with photos referenced and the ₦86k landing in damage expense under the storekeeper-plus-manager threshold rule.</p><p><b>Week 2 — the network breathes (Ch. 6).</b> The weekly Ibadan run: dispatch transfer moves 40 cartons PARA-500 plus twelve lines OUT of Central, IN to In-Transit, waybill referenced. Ibadan receives next morning AGAINST the dispatch: thirteen lines count clean; one line — cough syrup — counts 18 of 20. The receipt takes 18; the shortfall of 2 stays IN transit, visible, valued (₦21k), investigated same-day while the trail is hot: the waybill shows 20 loaded, the driver's second drop-point had no syrup on its manifest, the seals were intact at Ibadan... and the recount at Central finds 2 bottles mis-picked into the NEXT run's staging. Recovered: received into Ibadan on the following run's documents, transit back to zero. Total cost: one hour. The counterfactual — a one-step transfer — would have discovered nothing until Ibadan's next count surprised, and blamed the wrong branch.</p><p><b>Week 3 — the repack (Ch. 7).</b> The retail chain's blister-strip demand outgrows the subcontractor's monthly run, and small top-ups move in-house: a repack entry consumes 2 bulk cartons (200 strips' worth) plus packaging sleeves, produces 196 retail strip units carrying batch B-1152 and its expiry, and writes 4 damaged strips to the loss row — a 2% yield, logged, joining the trend the subcontractor's own 3% already lives on. The retail item is a DIFFERENT item by deliberate setup (own barcode, own price line) — the boundary conversation was had at design, which is why the entry is routine instead of an argument.</p><p><b>Week 4 — the appearance and the audit (Ch. 2, 4).</b> A shelf reorganisation surfaces a pallet of 6 cartons of vitamin syrup the ledger never knew — un-receipted during a chaotic delivery month before go-live. The Material Receipt enters it with a story: reason recorded, valued at the item's current valuation rate (₦4,200/carton — derived from the last honest purchase, not guessed), and flagged to the accountant for the offset. Month-end, the standing stock-entry audit runs: entries by purpose, value, and author — one page, five minutes, nothing anomalous: Material Receipts rare (one, explained), issues tracking operations, transfers clearing transit, the repack yield on trend. The boring page IS the control working.</p><p><b>The month's ledger read (Ch. 1-9).</b> Every internal movement carries a purpose, a room, a reason; the transit room slept at zero six nights in seven; value left only with signatures; and the one appearance had a story and a defensible rate. Module 4 will now test this ledger against the shelves — and because this month was disciplined, the count will be a confirmation, not a confession.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the second law", "13", "<p>The movement layer's scar tissue. Symptom → disease → fix.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — trading through the side door.</b> Symptom: Material Receipts at supplier-like rates; issues tracking sales. Disease: the boundary law broken (Ch. 2) — the match disarmed, suppliers unmeasurable, valuation typed, revenue leaving as expense. Fix: the lawful flows restored with their emergency speeds (the fast PO exists precisely so the side door has no excuse), the monthly stock-entry audit standing, and the historical mess reconciled deliberately with the accountant.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the issue as dustbin.</b> Symptom: consumption climbing with no operational story; count variances mysteriously scarce. Disease: shrinkage laundered through issues (Ch. 3) — differences buried where the reconciliation should have surfaced them, the shrinkage number destroyed. Fix: differences routed to module 4's instrument exclusively; issue references required; the issues-by-author-and-trend read that makes the laundering visible.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the unvalued appearance.</b> Symptom: found stock entered at zero (or at retail); margins swinging weirdly after. Disease: valuation improvised at the Material Receipt (Ch. 4) — the careless rate poisoning the costing stream for weeks. Fix: rates derived from the item's honest cost, the accountant's offset policy applied, value thresholds routing large receipts to approval — created value gets the same eyes as destroyed value.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — distance one-stepped.</b> Symptom: branch bins right eventually, wrong for hours or days; road losses surfacing as branch count variances; inter-branch blame. Disease: the one-step stretched over a custody gap (Ch. 5) — the ledger asserting arrivals that have not happened. Fix: the two-step with the transit room for anything with a road in it; the one-step reserved for moves where dispatch and receipt are one act.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the transit room that never empties.</b> Symptom: a standing in-transit balance nobody can itemise. Disease: receipts never entered, shortfalls never resolved, the room unread (Ch. 6) — the network's smoke detector unplugged. Fix: the aging read on the weekly rhythm, receipt-against-dispatch as unskippable routine, shortfalls investigated hot and resolved by the honest instruments — received, or written off from transit with governance.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — yields imagined.</b> Symptom: repacks always produce perfectly; counts after repacking surprise. Disease: yield dishonesty (Ch. 7) — losses unrecorded at the entry, surfacing later as custody variances. Fix: the loss row as routine, the yield trend watched, and the boundary check that the operation belongs in repack at all.</p><p><b>The second law.</b> Module 1: the warehouse tree is the map of custody. This module adds: <b>stock moves only by documents, and every movement names its purpose — where a trading counterparty exists, the trading chain carries the goods; where none does, the reason is the counterparty.</b> Receipts demand honest value, issues demand honest destinations, transfers demand honest custody at every moment of the journey, and transformations demand honest yields. A ledger whose movements all obey this law can be read like a diary; module 4 is about checking that diary against the world — the count — and a firm that kept this module's disciplines walks into that count unafraid.</p>"),
]

